os  they sent an email with a number saying  that they were available for assistance  and  i called and nobody's answering canada's  former ambassador to haiti says that's  because the embassy is likely dealing  with its own disarray no airplane uh no  gas to move vehicles from point a to  point b bad communication so there's  still there is some limit to what we can  do  and as foreigners flee the country this  former elections minister says canadians  and americans leaving will only lead to  more turmoil so we need the support of  the international community  to help us and we cannot do it by  ourselves and now a plea for other  countries to send more resources instead  of stripping them away from a country  that has little left to give kamal  karmali global news  still ahead the red tape facing foreign  trained doctors who want to help  canada's strained health care system  the pandemic has put additional strain  on canada's health care system which was  already facing a backlog of surgeries  then there are nurses burnt out and  leaving the profession at an alarming  rate and millions of canadians are  without a family doctor but a solution  some experts say is waiting in the wings  physicians trained in other countries  but as jamie morocco reports they say  they are still being sidelined by red  tape  motherhood isn't the reason dr saida  azam hasn't been able to work as a  family physician during the pandemic i  really felt bad that i couldn't  participate actively  it's that her years of training and  experience come from india and oman the  30 year old is just one of more than 13  thousand internationally trained  physicians who are not able to work as  doctors in canada  dr shafi buyon who immigrated here in  2010 says the retraining process is  confusing time consuming and costly we  need a policy change to accept and adopt  in this pandemic so that people can help  support  and canada could use the help as covid  stressed the staffing shortage further  some provinces turned to the military to  alleviate overwhelmed icu's bouyon says  it's time to invest in foreign doctors  global competition for health care  talent is now ongoing if canada failed  to take action these people will move  other country but the president of  canada's medical association says it's  not that simple and it's going to be  very difficult to have an approach  that's totally reliant on people coming  from overseas to solve our problems  because again the training and  experience of people can be quite  variable dr hassan mustafa has 10 years  as a surgeon in iraq and syria under his  belt and was a supervising physician at  one of the largest refugee camps in  kurdistan under the current system it  would take him five years to get  certified at 45 he says the clock is  ticking respect my experience  no  i work a lot before i have a lot of  experience experience he is willing to  put towards a less skilled job such as a  surgical assistant just to get into the  system  a zom meantime plans to make a career  change i'm looking forward to you know  apply for other causes like physician  assistant stepping back so she can step  forward into her new life here in canada  jamie morocco global news toronto  up next the indigenous guardians keeping  tabs on moose on cape breton island  they call themselves indigenous  guardians a network of 70 groups across  canada their formal network is only five  years old but the work they do goes back  hundreds of generations monitoring  managing and caring for land water  plants and animals on their territories  each guardian program is run locally as  distinct as the first nations they  represent our donna friesen got access  to one group with the big mon nation  tracking moose in cape breton also known  as unamagi  this as the buffalo is important to the  plains indians so is  the moose to the migma clifford has to  contend with a different kind of  conservation problem how many moose  would be  in the highlands here do you think the  last estimate was 2400 give or take so  from a migma perspective a healthy herd  is dependent on a healthy ecosystem so  2400 we think it's good because the  carrying capacity  of the ecosystem serves the population  better than having a hyper-abundant  population moose weren't always so  abundant a century ago the population  was wiped out another species from  alberta was introduced and with no  natural predators left it flourished so  today in the absence of wolves in  unamagi in nova scotia what's going to  keep the population in check is the  humans the human human interaction  without hunting moose would overrun the  highlands and over feed on the saplings  that regenerate the boreal forest  traditionally the migma would head  inland to hunt this time of year a way  of life disrupted by colonialism i have  the right to do this  but more importantly have the  responsibility and the migma fought so  hard for those rights they fought  incredibly hard for those rights  put  and how many were in your hunting party  that's where the guardians come in  hannah martin is with the confederacy of  mainland migma it's a really important  time for us as mgmaw people to be on the  land here in unamagi and  harvesting this resource it's more than  just a harvest it's everything for us  when a moose is harvested the antlers  are measured samples are collected  details from the hunters are recorded  all vital information to track and  monitor the health of the population  and you can catch donna's full  documentary tonight on the new reality  at 7 pm right here on global  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm robin gill tonight  your canada is kananaskis country in  alberta we would love to see your corner  of the country so please keep sending  your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]
 

